Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.76,0:00:04.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,Hello.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:04.30,0:00:04.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,Hello.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:04.83,0:00:07.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,Wow, this crowd looks amazing.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:07.15,0:00:12.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh, as Madison just said, my name is Tiffany Jansen and I'm going to be your moderator today.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:12.93,0:00:16.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,A little bit about me, I am the founder of Tiffen Tech.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:16.22,0:00:21.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I don't know about you, but I have been counting down the days for this panel.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.97,0:00:29.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,Humanoids have been in the the they we've seen a lot of advancements with them very recently and to
Dialogue: 0,0:00:29.08,0:00:35.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,be able to sit down with some of the leaders in this space and hear from them is truly incredible.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:35.34,0:00:38.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,Not only to learn where we are now but where we are headed.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:38.67,0:00:41.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,Let's start with a round of introductions.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:41.27,0:00:42.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'll start with Burnt.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:42.72,0:00:43.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,Sure.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:43.06,0:00:44.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,So my name is Burnt Barnick.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:44.73,0:00:52.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm the founder and CEO of 1X and we're on a mission to create an abundance of labor through these safe
Dialogue: 0,0:00:52.20,0:01:01.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,intelligent humanoids and we really believe that to truly get to intelligence these robots they need to live and learn among us.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:01.76,0:01:08.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's why we think consumer has to happen first to really be able to ex to experience all of the
Dialogue: 0,0:01:08.62,0:01:15.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,nuances that is kind of human life and then use that intelligence to be able to do useful labor in all other
Dialogue: 0,0:01:15.44,0:01:26.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,verticals down the line right hospitals elderly care retail factories logistics it's exciting y hi I am Deepak Puk and I'm
Dialogue: 0,0:01:26.14,0:01:32.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,CEO and co-founder of skilled AI at skilled what we are doing is we are building a general brain for robotics ICS.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:32.12,0:01:39.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,Our thesis is that we can have a single shared model because robotics is a field which is anyway scarce of data.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:39.12,0:01:43.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,We might as well use everything that we available from any platform, any task, any scenarios.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:43.53,0:01:50.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,So think of it as like a large scale foundation model you can use for any robot, any hardware, any task, any scenarios.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:50.72,0:01:55.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,uh Pros Feligaputo, the CTO at Agility Robotics and at Agility,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:55.41,0:02:02.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,our humanoid digit is made for work and we're bringing it out to manufacturing and logistics use cases today.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:02.31,0:02:10.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,We feel that the best way to get the technology out there and learn from it is to be able to get real customers and real deployments doing work.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:10.65,0:02:16.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's what we've been focused on and getting a robot out there and in the workforce.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:16.40,0:02:21.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um, Aaron Saunders, CTO of Boston Emics, uh, been working on humanoids before they were cool.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:21.46,0:02:26.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um, and at Boston, you know, our mission has been, uh, um, the same for a long time.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:26.67,0:02:27.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's to make robots real.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:27.85,0:02:30.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um, and we've shipped a couple thousand robots.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:30.26,0:02:33.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,The humanoid is the the latest, uh, kind of announcement for us.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:33.41,0:02:39.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh, and we're really wanting to bring a product to market that, uh, can do real useful work, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:02:39.57,0:02:44.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, to do work that, uh, um, uh, removes people from dirty, dull, dangerous things.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:44.44,0:02:47.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's the thing that we've been up to uh for a long time and
Dialogue: 0,0:02:47.31,0:02:50.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think there's more work to do yet but we're pretty excited about where we're going.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:50.97,0:02:52.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Hi everyone, I'm Jim Fan.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:52.64,0:02:57.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh I'm the co-lead of Nvidia Gear Lab and also project is Groot.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:57.52,0:03:04.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh Groot is Nvidia's moonshot initiative at building u the foundation model the robot brain for
Dialogue: 0,0:03:04.24,0:03:11.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,humanoid robots and Groot also represents our strategy for the next generation computing platform for physical AI.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:11.19,0:03:14.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we're also on a mission to democratize physical AI.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:14.54,0:03:21.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,In fact, yesterday at Jensen's uh keynote, we announced open sourcing of the Groot N1 model,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:21.16,0:03:25.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,uh which is the world's first open humanoid robot foundation model.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:25.84,0:03:30.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's only two billion parameters and um it punches above its weights.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:30.72,0:03:37.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,You are basically holding the world's state-of-the-art autonomous humanoid intelligence in the palm of your hand.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:37.84,0:03:46.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and um I would also like to say that um just like everyone else on this panel um I started working robotics before it was sexy.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:46.72,0:03:49.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and today I see the full house here.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:49.76,0:03:52.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I'm just really really glad that they become sexy today.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:52.80,0:03:53.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,So thank you all so much.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:53.86,0:04:00.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,All of you made my day.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:00.80,0:04:02.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,Oh, thank you all for being here.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:02.06,0:04:04.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,I know we're all excited.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:04.24,0:04:12.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,We had a call before our panel obviously and in that conversation I can't recall who exactly it was but someone
Dialogue: 0,0:04:12.21,0:04:22.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,shared on the call that robotics is the oldest application of AI and historically it's moved the slowest not the case
Dialogue: 0,0:04:22.22,0:04:28.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,anymore I would say and what has changed I think I I raised that question in that call yeah so um
Dialogue: 0,0:04:28.71,0:04:37.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,well I think the biggest change is Jensen now is paying attention to robotics Jensen's got a Jensen's got a Midas touch,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:37.19,0:04:37.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,right?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:37.58,0:04:41.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,Any field he puts his finger on is going to scale exponentially.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:41.46,0:04:44.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we call Jensen scaling law, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:44.34,0:04:46.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,So jokes aside,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:46.13,0:04:56.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think uh robotics one of the oldest field as long as AI itself existed and the reason that robotics is so hard um is because the Moravex paradox.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:56.80,0:05:05.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what this paradox says is the things that are easy for humans are actually really hard for machines and vice versa right the things
Dialogue: 0,0:05:05.12,0:05:12.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,that we kind of u find extremely hard like creative writing may actually not be that hard for machines and that's why the LMS like the
Dialogue: 0,0:05:12.45,0:05:21.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,NLP right computer vision is being solved much better than robotics these days so we are facing this paradox right now and now what has changed
Dialogue: 0,0:05:21.20,0:05:31.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,I would say a couple of aspects one is uh on the model side um because of large foundation models like Loom's the Chach GPT moment.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:31.50,0:05:37.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now we have models that can do reasoning and we have multimodal models that understands computer vision
Dialogue: 0,0:05:37.76,0:05:43.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,right open vocabulary understanding of the 3D visual world much much better than what we had before
Dialogue: 0,0:05:43.84,0:05:49.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,and these are necessary but insufficient conditions to solve robotics like you got to solve vision uh
Dialogue: 0,0:05:49.77,0:05:54.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,to have a really good vision system before you even talk about having a general purpose robot.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:54.96,0:06:01.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I think the rest of models are becoming really really good that we can start to tackle robotics uh a lot more systematically.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:02.01,0:06:07.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,So that's number one and number two what has changed is uh on the data side.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:07.14,0:06:15.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,So you know unlike Loom and I'm quoting Ilia Sask here he says the internet is the fossil fuel right for AI.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:15.21,0:06:23.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh well robotics doesn't even have the fossil fuel at least for LMS you can download text you can scrape text from Wikipedia.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:23.62,0:06:26.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,Where do we scrape motor control?
Dialogue: 0,0:06:26.13,0:06:30.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,Where do we scrape uh all of those like robot trajectories from the internet?
Dialogue: 0,0:06:30.31,0:06:31.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can't find it anywhere.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:31.84,0:06:33.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, we got to generate data.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:33.45,0:06:35.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,We got to collect data at scale.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:35.42,0:06:43.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I think the advent uh of simulation of GPU accelerated simulation really makes these problems more tractable because
Dialogue: 0,0:06:43.44,0:06:50.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,now you can generate like 10 years worth of training data in maybe three hours worth of compute time.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:50.27,0:06:54.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,So that really takes us beyond this data paradox.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:54.15,0:07:00.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,So that's number two and number three is on hardware side and we have uh some of the most extraordinary uh founders on
Dialogue: 0,0:07:00.97,0:07:06.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,some of the best um robot hardware we have ever seen and I think hardware has become a lot better and also a
Dialogue: 0,0:07:07.02,0:07:14.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,lot cheaper right like like this year we have seen hardware um in range of maybe 40k that's the price of a car
Dialogue: 0,0:07:14.90,0:07:24.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,um and back in 2001 NASA built Robonaut one of the first major humanoids and it's like $1.5 million in 2001 USD Right.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:24.09,0:07:31.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, it's finally become becoming affordable that um uh it will become mainstream very quickly.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:31.60,0:07:33.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Erin, I'd love to hear from you on this.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:33.20,0:07:36.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,You in your intro, you mentioned you've you were in robotics before it was cool.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:36.80,0:07:38.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, what do you think has changed?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:38.51,0:07:41.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, I think um that was a a lot of terrain.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:41.12,0:07:44.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, let me try to pick away at some some some pieces there.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:44.49,0:07:49.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um I do think the the kind of closure of the sim tore gap is a big deal, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:49.45,0:07:54.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I think for a long time uh the robotics community kind of struggled with
Dialogue: 0,0:07:54.45,0:08:00.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,creating a uh um a simulation environment that represented physics properly and that was computationally efficient.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:01.00,0:08:01.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,Right?
Dialogue: 0,0:08:01.28,0:08:05.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we could create very complex models that did a very good job of representing
Dialogue: 0,0:08:05.75,0:08:09.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,the physical world but we couldn't run them at real time or faster than real time.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:09.52,0:08:13.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I think for me the the biggest change has probably been the ability to
Dialogue: 0,0:08:13.60,0:08:18.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,represent the physics of the real world in greater than real time which lets you accelerate
Dialogue: 0,0:08:18.17,0:08:22.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,how many simulations you can explore and how you can use those simulations to develop
Dialogue: 0,0:08:22.76,0:08:27.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,new AI and then you know the the the commoditization of so many bits and pieces.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:27.05,0:08:31.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I think we we probably need to give a huge amount of credit to some adjacent industries right.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:31.86,0:08:37.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,So consumer electronics have developed batteries and cameras that that that like
Dialogue: 0,0:08:37.22,0:08:42.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know the the technology for perception for seeing the world for computing.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:42.12,0:08:51.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,uh when I look back you know even 101 15 years you know most of the robot was full of PCBAs and wires and had a very small amount
Dialogue: 0,0:08:51.62,0:08:55.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,of battery capacity and that's completely changed right we can put a huge amount
Dialogue: 0,0:08:55.40,0:08:59.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,of compute in we can put tiny sensors in uh they're power efficient so I
Dialogue: 0,0:08:59.75,0:09:06.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,think the commoditization of the the components and I don't think it's so much about low cost I know that's a big focus right now but I think the
Dialogue: 0,0:09:06.98,0:09:11.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,reason we're seeing the era of the robotic startup is because there's a global supply
Dialogue: 0,0:09:11.36,0:09:15.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,chain full of really important pieces that you can put together as a puzzle piece.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:15.18,0:09:21.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so we've elevated the robotics community from the people trying to design every cog to people who
Dialogue: 0,0:09:21.58,0:09:26.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,can put those things together as a puzzle and you and and basically be operated at a higher level.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:26.62,0:09:31.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,So now we have companies that are operating at the intelligence level that are developing applications
Dialogue: 0,0:09:31.27,0:09:36.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,rather than spending all that same amount of capital and energy just making a physical machine stand.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:36.96,0:09:37.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,Exactly.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:37.57,0:09:37.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:37.98,0:09:46.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,I can add something to I think what Jim's point initially uh about uh I think you put it very well about all the differences that have changed.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:46.26,0:09:49.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh I want to add that AI was not just the first application.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:50.02,0:09:53.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,Sorry, robotics was not the first application of AI, it is what AI is like.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:53.85,0:09:58.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,If you look at the original documents of Turing when he talked about AI, it was for robotics.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:58.29,0:10:06.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,He was like you should make something that learn instead of building like an adult make something that learns like a child and then it can grow.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:06.08,0:10:09.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can put the same robot in a classroom it will grow to be adult over time.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:09.69,0:10:18.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's a fascinating thought and he had this thought in 1950s right because language vision all of these things are cool but if you look
Dialogue: 0,0:10:18.38,0:10:26.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,at the nature they come much later in timeline compared to physical action like for instance LLMs we are training on the data it's from
Dialogue: 0,0:10:26.93,0:10:33.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,maybe last 100 years 200 years let's say thousand we are not training more than thousand years of data humans have been around for more
Dialogue: 0,0:10:33.39,0:10:37.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,than thousand years so it's not that language led to intelligence it
Dialogue: 0,0:10:37.51,0:10:41.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,was the infrastructure already existed Our brain actually existed which came through physical
Dialogue: 0,0:10:41.92,0:10:49.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,reasoning which is why the impact is so big like if you you don't have to explain a person to any person what robotics is
Dialogue: 0,0:10:49.21,0:10:57.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,you can feel it because we do physical task every other day every company gets impacted by robotics and that's the main deal so what
Dialogue: 0,0:10:57.42,0:11:04.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,has changed apart from all the factors that Jim mentioned like these are technical details that is yes but what has changed is completely how
Dialogue: 0,0:11:04.34,0:11:13.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,we approach robotics robotics so far has been the field of controls Controls was driving robotics until I would say even 3 4 years ago.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:13.20,0:11:18.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,Right now for those who have been in this area for a long time, they would know controls was not designed for robotics.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:18.32,0:11:25.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh like control had its really uh limelight during World War II for like flying planes, missiles and everything.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:25.39,0:11:29.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then you had the craze for robotics began because of because of touring.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:29.49,0:11:30.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then people like what do we use?
Dialogue: 0,0:11:31.00,0:11:33.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, let's use controls which were bu which was built for that.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:33.70,0:11:37.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that stuck around that stuck around for decades for 70 years.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:37.51,0:11:39.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,But it's not in the same spirit.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:39.51,0:11:40.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's not childlike learning.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:40.77,0:11:47.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,In child, you are not teaching them calculus first to learn how to walk like figure out your joint movements and then learn walk.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:47.70,0:11:49.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,You are learning by experience.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:49.52,0:11:53.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,So learning by experience is the main change that has happened.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:53.44,0:11:56.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now we are seeing all the sh all the shift happening.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:56.27,0:11:59.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and I mean BD just released a video today learning by experience.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:59.66,0:12:02.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,So that is the I would say one major change has happened.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:02.15,0:12:09.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,Instead of programming experience from that we have gone to learning by experience which is a major shift how we think about robotics.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:09.49,0:12:11.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,I want to actually double click on that.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:11.94,0:12:18.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I think for me right and I also been been in the field for long enough to be in the classical controls for
Dialogue: 0,0:12:18.27,0:12:27.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,team but um a large part of what happened was the internet right if you think about it like it's it's this enormous human experiment
Dialogue: 0,0:12:27.13,0:12:35.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,of like 30 close to 30 years of everyone around the world contributing to creating this enormous source of data so we can train an
Dialogue: 0,0:12:35.49,0:12:41.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,AI which is completely magical and now what we're going to do is we're just going to ask all of you to do the same
Dialogue: 0,0:12:41.28,0:12:48.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,again the next 30 years just go around and be robots and no we're not going to do that but we had that data and
Dialogue: 0,0:12:48.88,0:12:57.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,therefore that is what actually moved AI forwards even though it started in robotics and to me now what it's all about is how do
Dialogue: 0,0:12:57.32,0:13:06.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,we use that data that exists to bootstrap to where your robot can do something useful because at that point you can start to learn
Dialogue: 0,0:13:06.51,0:13:13.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,in the real world where where intelligence really comes from right but you have to get to where it is to a certain extent useful.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:13.52,0:13:18.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,So when I say go grab me a Coke in the fridge, if the robot's able to do so half the time,
Dialogue: 0,0:13:18.28,0:13:22.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,then we have a feasible path to getting there because now we just need to say,
Dialogue: 0,0:13:22.33,0:13:29.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,okay, that worked, that didn't work, and we need to run it enough times and it'll get really good at getting me a coke in the fridge.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:29.23,0:13:35.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I think that is what we're seeing now with the advent of all of these multimodal LLMs is that you you can't
Dialogue: 0,0:13:35.92,0:13:43.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,really solve robotics or intelligence in general I even think uh through this approach but you can get to where your system is
Dialogue: 0,0:13:43.44,0:13:52.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,useful enough that you can create a very efficient data flywheel at scale that doesn't require you to teleoperate the robots for everything
